 bilateral  meetings from with the Cambodia and my  and my I wondered did you hear anything  today that would avoid both those  countries losing their special trade  status under everything but arms thank  you I had bilateral meetings with both  they are in different stages with  Cambodia we have taken the decision to  start the process of temporary  withdrawal of the ABA trade preferences  I've discussed with the Prime Minister  and this team what are our expectations  for what should happen for this not to  go on any particular the improvement of  the political Democratic climates I  would say in particular there is a lot  of concern in Europe but not only in  Europe about the dissolution in November  last year of the main opposition party  and in general terms the narrowed  Democratic space for political  opposition and civil society we  discussed this I cannot say that we find  solution we found solutions to any of  these issues but the European Union  approach is always that of engaging and  and having a dialogue especially when  problems arise we agreed that our teams  would work together starting in this  days and in the weeks to come to go  through a long list of issues that we  have raised together with our decision  of suspending the trade preferences and  I am always hopeful you know me that  positive change could come so the work  will continue with Myanmar it's a  different stage we are in the process of  sending in the coming weeks a mission in  Myanmar to discuss the issues that  worries us related to in particular the  situation in the rakion State for which  as you know the European Union has been  very active not only on the military  incites to support the Ringo's in  Bangladesh but also swear but also on  the political level trying to support  the full implementation of the  memorandum of understanding that was  signed bilateral between Myanmar and  Bangladesh and also the mo use there  were signs with the UNHCR and the UN and  UNDP I discussed this also with filippo  grandi  in the recent weeks I encourage the  authorities of Myanmar to fully  implement the memorandums they have  signed the agreements they've signed to  work in particular on accountability  because the results of the you are an  independent international fact-finding  mission are extremely serious and  worrying and they have to respond to  this kind of allegations and show  willingness to have accountability  transparency and to address the issues  that are raised there so we will  continue to accompany Myanmar in on its  path towards democracy it's a difficult  process we always knew it our support to  the democratization of the country will  not stop now but this doesn't mean that  we would close our eyes on violations  that the international community see as  worrying but on the EBA on the trade  preferences the decision has not been  taken yet but obviously there are rules  that we need to follow and you know that  our trade preferences have the respect  for human rights at their core so I hope  that there will be science on the  Myanmar authorities site that could  allow us to avoid that decision to be  taken in the future one final question  because unfortunately we don't need to  move on Thank You Deborah Haynes from  Sky News it's a question for foreign  minister Kang obviously South Korea has  a comprehensive free trade agreement  with the EU could you give us a sort of  realistic sense for people back in the  UK  once the UK leaves the European Union  will will the UK government be able to  achieve the same level of free trade  agreement with South Korea will it  actually achieve an even better terms  with South Korea or given that the UK is  a single country as opposed to a big  bloc like the EU well actually the terms  of the free trade agreement between  South Korea and the UK beyond uncertain  worst terms thank you well I won't go  into hypotheticals but we're very keen  to maintain the excellent relationship  that we've enjoyed with the UK  part of the European Union the UK is our  second largest trading partner in the  European Union under the FTA that's been  very beneficial to both sides and I  think I can safely say that on both  sides the UK and us were very keen to  maintain that level of collaboration  obviously this requires a lot of work a  lot of contingency planning if I may put  it that way but within our own  government but in collaboration with our  counterparts in the UK we are already  having serious discussions about this  and my president had an excellent  discussion with Prime Minister made  today thank you if I can if I can remind  us all though that as long as the UK is  part of the European Union they cannot  negotiate nor conclude any additional to  any separate trade agreements and this  includes the transition periods for us  to be all aware of what we're talking  about with this I have to conclude this  press conference but thank you very much  for coming and there is also ample  material available including the chairs  statement

